  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nDiurnally driven cumulus clouds should dissipate this evening with\nthe loss of daytime heating. In the meantime, a few sprinkles are\npossible with any deeper cumulus that develop. particularly across\nnorth-central Wisconsin.\n\nMostly clear skies tonight will allow for some fog development\nacross north-central and portions of central Wisconsin late\ntonight into Friday morning. Any fog that develops would burn off\nshortly after sunrise Friday. Meanwhile, a backdoor cold front \nmay bring some clouds and sprinkles (10-15% chance) to far \nnortheast and eastern Wisconsin tonight as the front slides \nsouthwest.\n\nHigh pressure will slide east Friday night, allowing for return\nflow across the region. A series of weak shortwaves embedded in a\nflattened mid level flow will bring periodic chances for showers\nand thunderstorms to the area through the early part of next week.\nDespite the smattering of POPs in this portion of the forecast,\nthere will likely be periods of dry weather at times. However,\ngiven the model discrepancies it is difficult to pinpoint these\ndry versus wet periods. Although it is a bit early to determine\nsevere weather potential, early indications from the NBM\nshow instability through at least the weekend fails to crack 1000\nJ/kg. Therefore, a widespread severe weather outbreak through the\nweekend does not appear likely. There are indications from the\nmodels that the region could be in or on the periphery of moderate\nto heavy rainfall from the weekend through the early part of next\nweek. However, nailing down the specifics at this point given the\nuncertainty in shortwave timing and placement remains problematic.\n\nTemperatures will generally be around normal through the first\npart of the weekend, rising to a few degrees above normal for the\nsecond half of the weekend into the middle of next week.",
